  "subtitle": "The first telescope you point by keying a catalog number into a red-lit keypad",
  "why_scout_cares": "The Meade LX200 (1992, ~$2,000) was the first computer-controlled 'GoTo' telescope priced for the ordinary amateur: a Schmidt-Cassegrain on a motorized altazimuth mount driven by a 16-key hand controller with a 2-line LCD and a database of 64,359 celestial objects. You press an Object key, type a catalog number (M31, NGC 891, or 901–909 for planets), press ENTER then GO TO, and the mount slews up to 8°/second and settles the object in the eyepiece. The interface was designed for the dark: red-LED backlighting that preserves night vision, speed-graded manual control (SLEW / FIND / CNTR / GUIDE), and beep feedback. The LX200 protocol over RS-232 became the de-facto standard for amateur telescope control. The museum's other instruments (Tektronix 7854) are lab devices; the LX200 is the museum's first database-driven consumer instrument — pointing a 40-pound telescope by typing a number.",
  "search_terms": [
    "Meade LX200 1992 computerized GoTo telescope hand controller database",
    "LX200 Classic keypad hand controller 16-key 64359 objects RS-232 protocol",
    "Meade Instruments John Diebel LX200 history amateur astronomy computer"
  ],
